WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:06.799
The transformer architecture powers most of
the impressive recent breakthroughs in AI.

00:00:06.799 --> 00:00:13.570
The transformer is behind systems like ChatGPT,
Vision Transformers, Image Generators, AlphaFold

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:18.590
2 for predicting protein folding and many
others. So, if you are interested to learn

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:24.949
about the transformer, this is the right video
for you. We already made a video explaining

00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:31.199
the transformer, but it was one of our first
videos and I can do it so much better now.

00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:38.010
Also, there we did not spend enough time explaining
self-attention, which we will do better this

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.010
time.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:44.680
So here we go, with the remastered explanation
of the transformer architecture!

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:50.160
Transformers can work with ANY kind of data,
and by that, I mean text, images, speech,

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.680
and so on,
as long as we represent the data as a set

00:00:54.680 --> 00:01:01.010
of vectors. However, it is not always straightforward
to do this, as for example text does not naturally

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:06.090
come as a sequence of vectors. That means,
before we can look at the inner workings of

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:12.430
the transformer, we need to understand how
to represent inputs as vectors. So, let’s

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:18.500
look at two examples: text and images.
For text, we do the so-called tokenization

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:25.270
where we take a sequence of words and decompose
it with the tokenizer into subwords from a

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:31.350
predefined vocabulary, for example by following
whitespaces and breaking down compound words

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:36.550
into their components. If you want to know
more about tokenization, check out our video

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:37.640
on this.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:43.420
Then these subwords all get assigned a unique
vector. The vectors could be initialized randomly

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:49.540
or even better: with word embeddings! Word
embeddings work after the idea that distances

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:56.060
between embeddings represents word similarity
(a word is defined by the company it keeps)

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:00.330
and words that are semantically more similar
are initialized with vectors close in the

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:02.799
high dimensional vector space.

00:02:02.799 --> 00:02:08.170
You can easily download such word embeddings
as they are precomputed by counting how often

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:16.989
words appear next to other words in text and
a neural network learns to assign two words

00:02:16.989 --> 00:02:23.610
similar embeddings if they both have the same
neighbors. You can learn more about word embeddings

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:24.920
in our previous video.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:30.601
Now, that we know how to represent text, let’s
think about how to represent images. Images

00:02:30.601 --> 00:02:37.340
are more naturally represented as vectors,
or at least matrices, which are high dimensional

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:38.340
vectors:

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:43.099
An image is composed of three matrices, where
each matrix tells us for the red, green and

00:02:43.099 --> 00:02:46.930
blue channels what the light intensity of
that color

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:50.069
is in the corresponding pixel.

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:55.569
One could take the rows of each matrix and
write them one after the other to get vectors.

00:02:55.569 --> 00:03:01.180
But this would result in a lot of vectors
and transformers are much slower with many

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:05.040
vectors (as will become clearer later in this
video).

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.910
So what people do instead,
is to divide images into patches and apply

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:16.790
to each patch the same linear neural network
layer that trains together with the transformer,

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:23.909
to find the right weights that sensibly change
the dimensionality of p by p patches to a

00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:28.150
d times 1 matrix, which is a d dimensional
vector.

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:34.200
To summarize, the prerequisite of transformers
is that whatever the input, we must first

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.569
decide for a way to represent this input with
vectors.

00:03:38.569 --> 00:03:43.379
All neural networks, including the transformer,
process these vector representations into

00:03:43.379 --> 00:03:48.920
better and better representations with each
layer, until the solution for the task is

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:54.650
obvious (or linearly separable, if we want
to use jargon).

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:59.630
But compared to other neural networks, the
transformer does this processing in a specific

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:05.180
way, as following: Let’s suppose we have
an input sequence, here of text. And the task

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:10.629
is for example to predict which token comes
next, or whether the sentence expresses a

00:04:10.629 --> 00:04:16.680
positive or a negative sentiment, or any other
classification task we can think of.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:23.020
We take our input sequence, represent it as
vectors with word embeddings. One Transformer

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:29.139
layer takes in this sequence, updates the
vectors and outputs as many vectors as it

00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:34.650
had in the input and preserves the dimensionality
of the vectors.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:40.310
But to do something meaningful with this transformer,
we need to add special tokens, for example

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:46.509
a classification token at the end of the sequence.
This special token goes through the transformer

00:04:46.509 --> 00:04:54.509
in the same way as the other tokens.
But it’s special because to its output representation,

00:04:54.509 --> 00:05:00.250
we usually append a linear classification
layer that classifies from a list of words,

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:07.169
called the vocabulary, which tokens comes
next. And if we are trying to classify, it

00:05:07.169 --> 00:05:11.470
assigns probabilities to these classes.

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:17.040
And note that this is a simple classification
layer, or mathematically it is just a matrix

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:23.090
multiplication that happens here, which geometrically
corresponds to drawing a separation line in

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:28.180
the high dimensional space the word vectors
live in. In other words, the solution here

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:33.160
should be already obvious as prepared by the
transformer, such that we can tell fitting

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:37.180
classes from unfitting classes just by drawing
a line.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:41.949
During training, the transformer processes
the input, gives output vectors and we run

00:05:41.949 --> 00:05:46.430
the classification layer on the special tokens
and get the assigned class.

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:51.741
We compare the assigned prediction to the
expected one from the dataset, compute the

00:05:51.741 --> 00:05:58.000
loss value and backpropagate the loss value
and update the internal parameters of the

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:02.691
classification layer and the transformer layer
to values that minimize the loss, thus give

00:06:02.691 --> 00:06:08.199
better classification results next time.
Okay, but what happens in this mysterious

00:06:08.199 --> 00:06:12.630
box we call “transformer”?
Well, it is composed of multiple transformer

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:13.680
layers.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:19.900
One transformer layer contains two things:
One of them is not so much, it is just the

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:27.050
same feed forward network, also called MLP
sublayer, acting on every input token.

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:33.669
Such an MLP sublayer takes the input representation,
applies a dense layer with GeLU activation

00:06:33.669 --> 00:06:35.699
that doubles the dimension.

00:06:35.699 --> 00:06:40.449
Then another dense layer with GeLU activation
scales down the dimension again. And it is

00:06:40.449 --> 00:06:46.960
the same MLP layer, with the exact same weights
we apply to each input token embedding.

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:52.759
Ok, let’s see what we have. A bunch of MLP
layers processing each token independently

00:06:52.759 --> 00:06:59.069
of the others. This is suboptimal, because
see that this word representation? It does

00:06:59.069 --> 00:07:01.870
not even know that there are other words next
to it.

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:07.590
And it is even worse for the [CLS] token,
that should aggregate and summarize the sentence

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:12.930
information if we are to use it for classification,
but it has no connection to the sentence tokens

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:13.930
at all!

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:17.810
While the transformer layer saves a lot of
compute time because all these MLP layers

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:23.080
compute their output in parallel, we need
a way to communicate information in the context

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:28.729
of the sequence, so that the word “works”
is informed of the existence and semantics

00:07:28.729 --> 00:07:32.120
of its neighbour “attention”, for example.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:39.370
Luckily, this is what the self-attention sublayer
is for: to let information flow within the

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:44.639
context of the sequence, from one embedding
to its neighbors. In a nutshell, the attention

00:07:44.639 --> 00:07:50.720
layer computes how much of the representation
of each of all neighbours we need to add to

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:55.990
compute a new token representation, which
is the outcome of the self-attention layer.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:08:01.870
By the way, we will be using attention and
self-attention here synonymously. But if you

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:06.580
are wondering what the difference between
them is: self-attention is when we compute

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:13.870
importances of the elements of a sequence
to the elements in the same sequence.

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:18.310
Attention is more general because we compute
the importance of the elements of one sequence

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:24.650
to the elements in another sequence. For example,
you can see here the self-attention of “it”

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:30.360
on the left, and the attention of “ihn”
on the right. “Ihn” is an element from

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.940
a sequence different to the one above it.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:38.360
Now, how does the attention layer compute
these importances exactly?

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:45.640
Well, it is a bit complicated in the sense
that it is a pile of linear algebra that uses

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:51.690
the loss function to adapt the entries of
weight matrices during training to make them

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:57.029
work well in inference. But neural networks
are never anything else others than huge piles

00:08:57.029 --> 00:09:00.970
of linear algebra
so strap yourself onto your chair because

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:05.820
we will try to explain the attention computation
as clear as possible.

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:10.920
Self-attention does the following:
It takes the input vectors and applies 3 different

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:15.769
linear transformations to produce the keys,
queries and values vectors.

00:09:15.769 --> 00:09:22.110
This means that for the queries, it multiplies
the Query matrix to the input vector and this

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:27.750
results in a query vector. This query matrix
is randomly initialized before training and

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:33.700
gradient descents adapts its values during
backpropagation to make them the right ones

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:39.570
that reduce the loss on the training data.
And the same query matrix applies to all inputs

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:46.120
to get query vectors for all of them. As for
the keys, we simply have another matrix called

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:52.529
the key matrix which is differently initialized
from the query matrix that also multiplies

00:09:52.529 --> 00:09:58.310
to the input vector to produce a key vector.
And to produce the value vectors, we multiply

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:00.399
a Value matrix to the input.

00:10:00.399 --> 00:10:07.550
So, in summary, we have three different matrices,
all initialized randomly that linearly transform

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:12.019
the input in different ways.
Now what is self-attention further doing to

00:10:12.019 --> 00:10:17.470
these different vectors it has just produced?
Let’s suppose we are calculating the attention

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:22.700
for the input token “works” to all other
tokens in the sequence, including itself.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:30.390
It works the same for the other tokens too.
First, we compute the scalar product between

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:35.959
the query vector of the token of interest,
and the keys of every other vector. Then we

00:10:35.959 --> 00:10:42.150
divide by the square root of the dimension
of the key vectors, so square root of 3. Then

00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:45.510
we apply the softmax over all these values.

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:50.149
We can interpret these
softmax scores to be measuring how important

00:10:50.149 --> 00:10:56.510
each token in the input is for the token “works”.
So, the token “Attention” is 13% important

00:10:56.510 --> 00:11:03.550
for “works”, “works” is 78% important
for itself and the [CLS] token is 7% important.

00:11:03.550 --> 00:11:09.120
Now it gets interesting. To get the final
representation of “works”, we take the

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:16.970
sum over all value vectors weighted / multiplied
with the softmax result.

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:21.110
So this is what we meant before by saying
that attention combines the representations

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:26.149
of the input (the value vectors) weighted
by the importance score.

00:11:26.149 --> 00:11:32.660
Empirically it turns out that one set of attention
values in each layer is not enough to capture

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:34.370
the complexity of relationships in our data.

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:39.440
Think of it this way: the attention importance
scores define a graph where it tells us for

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:43.649
each token, of how important that token is
to all others.

00:11:43.649 --> 00:11:48.731
But one graph is not enough to model all existing
relationships, in the same way

00:11:48.731 --> 00:11:54.560
you can define your social network graph based
on how many friends you have, you can also

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:59.769
think of other types of connections, like
with whom of these people you work together.

00:11:59.769 --> 00:12:05.139
Or, with whom you share the same city.
There are multiple relationships and importances

00:12:05.139 --> 00:12:08.280
to be modelled given a set of tokens.

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:14.680
Therefore the idea of multi-head self-attention
is to let the network learn 3, or 8, or 12

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:20.589
attention patterns, instead of just one. So
we do not use one set of query, key and value

00:12:20.589 --> 00:12:26.620
matrices, but 3 of them and each set is called
an “attention head”. As we initialise

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:32.440
the key, query and value matrices all randomly,
they will start with different values in their

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:38.079
training process, will produce different query
vectors and they will usually capture different

00:12:38.079 --> 00:12:43.870
patterns that they detect in your data. One
head might focus on one pattern such as coreference

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:48.699
resolution, and another one on identifying
the subject in sentences. If you wonder how

00:12:48.699 --> 00:12:53.399
many attention heads you need, the answer
is that you are free to choose. It is a hyperparameter.

00:12:53.399 --> 00:13:01.300
The more, the better, but often you can not
use very many as you quickly run out of GPU

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:02.300
memory.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:05.110
Especially because attention scales quadratically
in time and memory.

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:09.829
So if you process a sequence that doubles
the size, you will need four times as much

00:13:09.829 --> 00:13:15.089
time to run and four times as much memory.
It is an active area of research to approximate

00:13:15.089 --> 00:13:20.010
attention with other operations that scale
linearly instead of quadratically, or to replace

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:25.220
it altogether with other operations that do
the job of mixing information between tokens.

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:29.070
If you are interested in this topic, please
watch our previous videos on this. But it

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:33.649
a nutshell, it’s fake news that attention
is all you need. You can replace it with other

00:13:33.649 --> 00:13:35.640
token mixing procedures too.

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:40.640
Now, let’s recap what we have so far and
what we still need for a full transformer.

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:45.279
We have our input embeddings, they go through
the self-attention layer that gives us representations

00:13:45.279 --> 00:13:50.060
that are informed on the fellow embeddings
in the sequence. Then they go through the

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:52.740
MLP layer all in parallel.

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:56.970
But so far, this transformer layer behaves
like our input sequence weren’t a sequence,

00:13:56.970 --> 00:14:01.120
but a set.
If we were to reorder the tokens, the transformer

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:05.589
would not change its outputs. The result of
the attention would still be the same as all

00:14:05.589 --> 00:14:10.490
operations there are commutative, please check
to convince yourself.

00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:16.450
And the Feed Forward network acts independently
of all the other tokens anyway. This is not

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:21.410
great that so far, the transformer gives us
the same output independently of the order

00:14:21.410 --> 00:14:23.110
of the input.

00:14:23.110 --> 00:14:28.730
Because images, text and sound are sequences
where order matters, we need a way to tell

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:33.170
the transformer layer that this is the first
token in the sequence, this is the second,

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:37.020
and so on.
And this is what positional embeddings do.

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:43.350
They are vectors that uniquely identify each
position, which we add to the input embeddings.

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:49.510
They work like house numbers to identify the
specific position of each house in a street

00:14:49.510 --> 00:14:54.230
address. How do we come up with the values
for the positional embeddings? Well, with

00:14:54.230 --> 00:14:59.519
certain rules or we can simply learn these
vectors as well during the training process

00:14:59.519 --> 00:15:03.709
of the transformer. If you want more details
about positional embeddings and the numerous

00:15:03.709 --> 00:15:08.570
ways to implement them, you can watch one
of our previous videos on this. Okay, now

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:14.290
we got this figured out, but there is one
more thing missing and the architecture is

00:15:14.290 --> 00:15:15.290
complete.

00:15:15.290 --> 00:15:20.759
The missing ingredient are the residual connections
which after the self-attention layer add the

00:15:20.759 --> 00:15:26.209
input of the self-attention layer, to its
output. A normalization operation reduces

00:15:26.209 --> 00:15:33.399
the values back again to the 0 to 1 range,
because otherwise, after each residual connection,

00:15:33.399 --> 00:15:38.620
with each layer, the values would get larger
and larger and larger… And the same thing,

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:45.259
of adding the input back to the output happens
around the MLP layer, here in green. The intuition

00:15:45.259 --> 00:15:51.420
behind residual connections is to make the
learning job easier for each layer. To arrive

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:57.759
at the solution, the network needs to transform
the inputs. But since it is allowed to keep

00:15:57.759 --> 00:16:04.230
the input through the residual connections,
each layer is forced to learn not the whole

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:11.649
transformation, but just the difference it
needs to add to arrive at the output.

00:16:11.649 --> 00:16:15.730
And residual connections become even more
important, as usually with deep neural networks,

00:16:15.730 --> 00:16:21.029
we usually do not use just one transformer
layer, but append another transformer layer

00:16:21.029 --> 00:16:27.360
to the output of the previous one, and another
layer, and so on. How many? It is a hyperparameter

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:33.389
and of course we are limited by the amount
of memory our GPUs have. The more the better,

00:16:33.389 --> 00:16:36.589
because with many layers, the transformer
gets more attempts to break down the problem

00:16:36.589 --> 00:16:42.420
and arrive at the solution, which is easier
than getting to the solution in one go with

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:48.079
just one layer. And residual connections help
when training such a long stack of layers,

00:16:48.079 --> 00:16:53.589
because during backpropagation, gradient signals
can get lost by propagating from the end to

00:16:53.589 --> 00:16:57.250
the beginning – very much like a whisper
in the telephone game -- like it is called

00:16:57.250 --> 00:16:58.250
in the US.

00:16:58.250 --> 00:17:02.740
Now, this was most of what you need to know
about transformer basics, since you now know

00:17:02.740 --> 00:17:08.189
the principles after which they predict the
next word, like GPT, or classify the whole

00:17:08.189 --> 00:17:11.070
sequence.
Another training procedure we left for the

00:17:11.070 --> 00:17:17.030
end, is the so called Masked Language Modelling
procedure used for transformers of the BERT

00:17:17.030 --> 00:17:22.970
family. There, we have a classifier token
that we use to classify whether two sentences

00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:28.660
belong together or not, but there is more:
15% of tokens in the sequence are chosen randomly

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:32.530
and masked out, and replaced with a special
[MASK] token.

00:17:32.530 --> 00:17:37.890
The training objective of BERT is then to
adapt its weights such that a linear mask

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:44.070
classification head can choose from the vocabulary
the word that we masked out in the input.

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:48.460
This masked language modelling procedure is
great to train classification transformers,

00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:56.200
or transformer encoders. Predicting the next
word is something for GPT-like models, so

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:57.750
transformer decoders.

00:17:57.750 --> 00:18:01.760
If you are wondering what the difference between
Transformers and Recurrent neural networks

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:07.870
(RNNs) is, let’s look at this in a simplified
view. While in Transformers, we use attention

00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:14.159
to communicate information in parallel from
each input token to every other token,

00:18:14.159 --> 00:18:22.450
RNNs process the first token, and use that
output as input together with the second token,

00:18:22.450 --> 00:18:28.900
to process the second token. Then the output
of the second token goes into the processing

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:33.750
of the third token, and so on. And you see
the problem: that we need to wait for the

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:38.570
second token to finish processing, so we can
start computing the third token. This means

00:18:38.570 --> 00:18:43.950
that RNNs train slower than Transformers.
So, when Transformers revolutionized NLP,

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:48.840
it’s because their architecture allowed
them to read the entire internet because they

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:54.880
could process tokens in parallel, while with
RNNs, nobody got to train onto the whole internet

00:18:54.880 --> 00:18:56.930
because it took so much time.

00:18:56.930 --> 00:19:00.970
We hope you liked this little introduction
to the transformer architecture and that you

